5.6 Root Cause Analysis (RCA)
Example: Excavator repeatedly overheats.
Why?	Finding
Why did the engine overheat?	Cooling capacity was insufficient
Why was cooling capacity insufficient?	Radiator airflow was restricted
Why was airflow restricted?	Dust and debris accumulated
Why did cleaning not happen?	Daily inspection was inadequate
Why was inspection inadequate?	Responsibility and checklist control were unclear

Corrective action: Clean the cooling system and inspect the radiator.

Preventive action: Introduce a shift-wise radiator inspection, cleaning frequency based on site dust conditions, supervisor sign-off and temperature trend monitoring.

RCA must be evidence-based. Do not write "operator mistake" as the root cause without investigation.

6. Spare parts inventory management — detailed depth
Spare inventory is one of the biggest controllable areas in P&M management. Too much stock blocks money; too little stock increases breakdown downtime.

Your objective is:

Right spare + right quantity + right location + right time + controlled cost.






















10











6.1 Spare parts classification
A. ABC analysis — based on annual consumption value
Category	General meaning	Management control
A	High annual consumption value	Tight control, frequent review
B	Medium annual consumption value	Regular review
C	Low annual consumption value	Simplified control

Example:
Spare	Annual consumption value	Category
Hydraulic pump	₹8,00,000	A
Engine overhaul kit	₹5,00,000	A
Hydraulic filter	₹1,20,000	B
Hose clamp	₹8,000	C

ABC categories should be calculated using the actual annual consumption value, not simply the unit purchase price.
B. VED analysis — based on criticality
Category	Meaning	Example
V — Vital	Equipment cannot safely or practically operate without it	Critical hydraulic pump
E — Essential	Important but limited alternatives may exist	Fuel injection component
D — Desirable	Non-critical or replaceable without immediate shutdown	Non-critical cabin accessory

C. FSN analysis — based on movement
Category	Meaning
F — Fast-moving	Frequently consumed
S — Slow-moving	Occasionally consumed
N — Non-moving	No movement over the defined period

D. HML analysis — based on unit price
- H = High price.
- M = Medium price.
- L = Low price.
Use ABC + VED + FSN + HML together. For example, a high-value, vital, slow-moving spare may deserve special management attention even if its annual consumption value is low.
