6.3 Reorder level, minimum and maximum stock
Use these formulas as a basic stock-control method:
\[
\text{Reorder Point}=(\text{Average Daily Consumption}\times\text{Lead Time})+\text{Safety Stock}
\]

Example:
- Average consumption = 2 filters/day.
- Supplier lead time = 10 days.
- Safety stock = 5 filters.
\[
ROP=(2\times10)+5=\boxed{25\text{ filters}}
\]

When stock reaches 25 filters, procurement should be triggered.

A simple inventory policy:
Parameter	Example
Minimum stock	5
Reorder point	25
Maximum stock	40
Average consumption	2/day
Lead time	10 days
Safety stock	5

This example assumes stable demand and supplier lead time. For critical, unpredictable or imported spares, use a more suitable service-level and risk-based model.
6.4 Inventory calculations
KPI	Formula
Stock value	Quantity × weighted average cost
Inventory turnover	Annual consumption value ÷ average inventory value
Stock accuracy	Correct physical stock ÷ book stock × 100
Stock-out rate	Stock-out incidents ÷ total spare requests × 100
Dead stock %	Non-moving stock value ÷ total stock value × 100
Purchase price variance	Actual purchase price − standard/reference price
Service level	Requests fulfilled on time ÷ total requests × 100

Example: Stock accuracy
- ERP book stock = 100 filters.
- Physical stock = 95 filters.
\[
\text{Stock Accuracy}=\frac{95}{100}\times100=95\%
\]

Investigate the difference: unposted issues, incorrect GRN, wrong part number, damaged stock or counting error.
6.5 Store management process
Spare parts lifecycle
1. Indent / request: Maintenance raises a spare requirement with equipment ID and job reference.
2. Stock verification: Store checks physical and ERP stock.
3. Approval: P&M and procurement approvals as per delegation.
4. Purchase: Supplier quotation, technical specification and PO.
5. GRN: Quantity, part number, quality and condition checked.
6. Storage: Bin location, QR/barcode, batch/serial and warranty captured.
7. Issue: Spare issued against equipment and maintenance order.
8. Consumption: Actual part used recorded in job card / ERP.
9. Return: Unused or defective material returned and accounted for.
10. Reconciliation: Physical count compared with ERP book stock.

