3. LOTO – Lockout and Tagout
Critical safety procedure
LOTO prevents accidental energisation or release of stored energy during maintenance.








hseblogger.com


3.1 Seven-step LOTO procedure
1. Identify energy sources: Electrical, hydraulic, pneumatic, mechanical, thermal, gravity and stored energy.
2. Notify affected persons: Inform operator, supervisor and maintenance team.
3. Shut down equipment: Follow the approved shutdown procedure.
4. Isolate energy: Switch off and isolate all relevant energy sources.
5. Lock and tag: Apply personal locks and identification tags.
6. Release stored energy and verify: Discharge pressure, block moving parts and prove zero energy using an appropriate test.
7. Perform work and restore safely: Remove tools, guards and personnel; each worker removes their own lock under the approved procedure.

Examples:
- Crusher: isolate electrical supply and prevent material or mechanical movement.
- RMC mixer: isolate power and secure against unexpected rotation.
- Excavator: lower attachments and release stored hydraulic energy according to the manual.
- Crane: isolate power and secure suspended or movable components.
- DG set: isolate generator and prevent accidental starting.
A stop button is not an energy-isolating device. An emergency stop alone does not make maintenance safe.

